[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Understanding the Cypress Command Queue and Closures"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"One of the most common pitfalls for developers transitioning to Cypress is treating its commands like synchronous JavaScript. Unlike standard asynchronous code using async/await or standard promises, Cypress commands do not execute immediately when they are encountered in your test script."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"The Cypress Command Queue"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"When you write Cypress code, you are actually building a queue of asynchronous actions. Commands like cy.get(), cy.click(), and cy.type() return nothing (or rather, undefined). They simply enqueue instructions to be run later by the Cypress test runner engine."},{"order":5,"type":"code","text":"cy.get('.user-profile').click();
cy.get('.notification').should('have.text', 'Success');"},{"order":6,"type":"paragraph","text":"In the example above, neither cy.get() runs immediately when the line is parsed. Instead, Cypress queues both commands and executes them sequentially in the background."},{"order":7,"type":"subheading","text":"The Role of Closures and the .then() Command"},{"order":8,"type":"paragraph","text":"Because Cypress commands operate in a separate queue, you cannot simply assign the result of a command to a standard JavaScript variable. To work with data yielded by a command, you must use closures via the .then() method."},{"order":9,"type":"code","text":"cy.get('.price-tag').then(($el) => {
  const priceText = $el.text();
  const numericPrice = parseFloat(priceText.replace('$', ''));
  expect(numericPrice).to.be.greaterThan(10);
});"},{"order":10,"type":"bullet_point","text":"The .then() command injects your callback function into the command queue."},{"order":11,"type":"bullet_point","text":"It waits for the previous command (.get()) to resolve before executing."},{"order":12,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Whatever your closure returns becomes the new subject yielded to the next command in the queue (unless it returns undefined)."},{"order":13,"type":"subheading","text":"Managing State with Aliases"},{"order":14,"type":"paragraph","text":"When you need to share data across multiple tests or separate command chains, closures alone may become difficult to manage. Cypress provides the .as() command to alias subjects for later use."},{"order":15,"type":"code","text":"cy.get('@user-token').then((token) => {
  cy.request({
    url: '/api/settings',
    headers: { Authorization: token }
  });
});"},{"order":16,"type":"subheading","text":"Best Practices for Avoiding Anti-Patterns"},{"order":17,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Avoid mixing standard synchronous JavaScript variables with Cypress subjects outside of .then() blocks."},{"order":18,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Do not attempt to use async/await syntax with Cypress commands; it is not supported and will break the queue."},{"order":19,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Embrace functional programming styles by chaining commands and leveraging .then() for side effects."}]
